---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PortraitReveal from '../components/PortraitReveal';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Cody Duke, product designer. Ten years turning complex problems into useful experiences, from research through delivery."
>
  <div class="shell about-page">
    <div class="about-grid">
      <h1 class="about-title">I design products,<br />not just screens.</h1>

      <div class="about-copy prose">
      <p>
        I’m a product designer with 10+ years of experience turning complex problems into useful,
        thoughtful experiences. I care about the full picture, from understanding the problem and
        shaping the strategy to designing, building, and getting the right solution into people’s
        hands.
      </p>
      <p>
        I earned a BFA in Graphic Design from BYU-Idaho in 2017 and started my UX career at
        FamilySearch, where I worked on experiences focused on engagement, retention, and discovery.
        Some of that work contributed to measurable results, including 95% more members and 107%
        more other patrons retained compared to the previous year, along with a campaign that
        reached 1.1 million patrons outside the US. Across 26 campaigns, we also collected 86,000
        feedback comments, 90% of which were positive.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 2019, I joined Entrata, where I spent seven years designing complex products for the
        property management industry. I eventually stepped into a Product Lead role, expanding my
        responsibilities beyond design to include research, product strategy, prioritization,
        prototyping, and close collaboration with an engineering team. I enjoyed being involved in
        the entire process, not just handing off designs and hoping for the best.
      </p>
      <p>
        During my time at Entrata, the way I worked changed significantly. As the company shifted
        toward AI-assisted product development, I started rethinking my own process. Over the past
        year and a half, I’ve built an AI-powered design workflow using tools like Figma Make and
        Cursor. I use them to move from research and ideas to working prototypes, documentation,
        and engineering collaboration much faster. The goal isn’t just to work faster. It’s to
        spend less time on busywork and more time thinking through the problems and making better
        decisions.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’m looking for a team that values curiosity, ownership, and good problem-solving. I enjoy
        ambiguous challenges, complex workflows, and products where understanding the people using
        them matters just as much as understanding the business behind them.
      </p>

      <h2>The polished stone</h2>
      <p>
        The stone on my homepage represents how I think about design.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most good products start out a little rough. There’s a problem, an idea, or a complaint,
        but it’s usually bigger and messier than it first appears. You have to work through it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Research helps uncover what’s actually going on. Sketches and workflows start giving the
        problem shape. Prototypes put ideas to the test, and feedback helps reveal what needs to
        change. Each iteration removes a little more of what’s unnecessary.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal is to create something that feels simple and intentional, not because the problem
        was simple, but because the work behind it was thoughtful.
      </p>
      <p>
        That’s the kind of design I enjoy doing.
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Own the problem.</strong> I like being involved from the initial question through research, strategy, design, and delivery.</li>
        <li><strong>Stay close to reality.</strong> Site visits, customer conversations, usability testing, and production data help keep decisions grounded in how people actually work.</li>
        <li><strong>Make thoughtful tradeoffs.</strong> Good design involves knowing what matters most, what can wait, and how we’ll know whether something worked.</li>
        <li><strong>Use the tools available.</strong> AI has become a big part of my workflow, helping me research, prototype, document, and collaborate more efficiently. The tools can accelerate the work, but the thinking and decisions still belong to me.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The path here</h2>
      <div class="about-path">
        <div>
          <h3>BYU-Idaho</h3>
          <p>BFA, Graphic Design · 2012–2017</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>FamilySearch</h3>
          <p>UX Design Intern · 2016</p>
          <p>UX Designer · 2017–2019</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Entrata</h3>
          <p>Senior Product Designer · 2019–2026</p>
          <p>Product Lead · Approximately 1 year</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Today</h3>
          <p>
            Building AI-assisted design workflows, creating coded prototypes, and exploring what
            product design looks like when the distance between an idea and a working product gets
            smaller.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>What collaborators say</h2>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          “I hired Cody onto my team and have seen him grow through the majority of his career, so I
          know his work well. He’s that rare designer/PM hybrid who doesn’t stop at the problem as
          stated. He keeps digging until he finds the root cause, and more than once that instinct
          changed what we built. Cody doesn’t design from opinion, he designs from evidence… I’d
          hire Cody again without hesitation.”
        </p>
        <footer>— Jeff Nicholson, Sr. Vice President of Product Design (Entrata)</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          “Cody is an amazing product manager with a strong eye for design. We partnered together to
          build a lot of stuff at Entrata and he always raised the bar to deliver exceptional products
          and features that our clients raved about for years to come. I hope we end up on the same
          team again!”
        </p>
        <footer>— Robert Jones, Principal Product Manager (Entrata)</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          “Cody is one of the most talented overall designers I’ve worked with. He can take seriously
          complicated flows and turn them into interfaces people can actually use without thinking
          twice.”
        </p>
        <footer>— Kyle Ashby, Product Designer (Entrata)</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          “I have had the privilege of managing Cody on our User Experience Design team. Cody is a
          talented designer that is thoughtful about crafting delightful experiences. He has worked on
          a variety of projects on our team and I always knew I could count on him to be dependable,
          thorough, and work well with his team. Cody took complex data and requirements and
          prototyped, usability tested, produced high fidelity designs to his development team with
          sometimes a very quick turnaround. He also acted as an art director with third-party design
          firms and was able to greatly improve the usability of experiences and our brand’s presence.”
        </p>
        <footer>— Michelle Barber, UX Design Manager (FamilySearch)</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          “Cody has been a great addition to our UX team. We hired him as an intern and were privileged
          to have him work for us full time. He is passionate about making designs work for our
          customers by using good design and testing. He works well with a number of stakeholders.
          Juggling the demands and needs of several marketing product managers, engineering and the
          customers. He is hungry for feedback and ways to improve, but he works independently to keep
          projects moving and shipping on time. Highly recommended.”
        </p>
        <footer>— Grant Skousen, Chief Empathy Officer (FamilySearch)</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          “Cody is passionate about helping uncover the right experience for patrons. He has created
          some very inspiring experiences that helped drive excellent engagement… [and] very strong
          patron feedback scores.”
        </p>
        <footer>— Cris Rees, Sr. Product Manager (FamilySearch)</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          “Cody has been a user experience design mentor for me since I joined his agile team,
          encouraging me and guiding me through this maze. He regularly and cheerfully designs
          successful online campaigns while fielding the constant stream of curve balls thrown his way
          during the process, making sure to achieve stakeholder goals and pushes even farther to
          achieve additional campaign successes they hadn’t even been thinking of.”
        </p>
        <footer>— Robb Perry (FamilySearch)</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          “Cody is a real craftsman when it comes to visual design. While I worked with him during my
          internship, I feel like my sense of visual aesthetic increased by receiving feedback and
          shadowing him. As a UX Designer, he is able to bring intention and human-centered design
          methodologies to bear in his work. By making sure to validate any solutions he had through
          meticulous testing he is able to marry stunning visuals with the intention of a proper
          product designer.”
        </p>
        <footer>— Caden Damiano, Software Designer (FamilySearch)</footer>
      </blockquote>
      </div>

      <aside class="about-aside">
        <PortraitReveal client:load src={withBase('/images/cody-duke.jpg')} alt="Portrait of Cody Duke" />
        <div class="contact-panel" id="contact">
          <h2>Get in touch</h2>
          <div class="contact-actions">
            <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>Send a message</a>
            <a href={site.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a>
            <a href={withBase('/resume')}>Resume</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
